    "locked": false,
    "maintainer_can_modify": true,
    "title": "net: receive TCP urgent data inline",
    "user": {
      "login": "darosior",
      "id": 22457751,
      "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjIyNDU3NzUx",
      "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/22457751?v=4",
      "gravatar_id": "",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/darosior",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/following%7B/other_user%7D",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/gists%7B/gist_id%7D",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/starred%7B/owner%7D%7B/repo%7D",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/events%7B/privacy%7D",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/darosior/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "site_admin": false,
      "name": null,
      "patch_url": null
    },
    "body": "There is currently nothing preventing us to receive TCP urgent data, which would never be returned by `recv()`. Linux's `poll()` is indifferent to this, but the `select()` socket monitoring may keep reporting it as pending on some platforms.\n\nThis would not prevent progress nor lead to a crash anymore after #36665, but it's still undesirable. Clean this up by setting `SO_OOBINLINE` on our sockets, so `recv()` returns any urgent data sent to us (and likely leads us to disconnect the peer).\n\nThis is a slight behaviour change on Linux, but i think a desirable one. First of all because if a peer is sending us funky data we might as well hear about it and disconnect them, but also because i think one lesson to draw from the recent [socket related security vulnerability](https://bitcoincore.org/en/2026/10/28/disclose-tcp-oom/) is that we should reduce the amount of custom logic for less well-tested platforms.\n\nFailing to set `SO_OOBINLINE` was conservatively made a warning and not a failure, because its absence does not prevent node operations, it's just annoying.",
